\subsection{Short English statement}
With the interaction and initial state known, does a particle density determine the driving potential and the resulting many-body evolution?

\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Gives explicit density-to-current-to-potential reconstruction and uniqueness modulo time gauge for a smooth positive one-particle density on a circle, with initial-state compatibility; does not settle interacting many-body reconstruction.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{Context and investigated scope.} The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{A reconstructible one-particle case.}
Take one particle on the circle $\mathbb R/L\mathbb Z$, with kinetic operator $-\tfrac12\partial_x^2$. Suppose a proposed density $n(x,t)$ is smooth, strictly positive, periodic, and has integral one at every time. Write a wave function as $\psi=\sqrt n\,e^{iS}$, allowing the real phase to have winding $S(L,t)-S(0,t)=2\pi k$, $k\in\mathbb Z$. The fixed nonvanishing initial wave function determines $k$.
The imaginary part of the Schr\"odinger equation is the continuity equation
\[
 n_t+\partial_x j=0,\qquad j=nS_x.
\]
Define $F(x,t)=\int_0^x n_t(y,t)\,dy$. Every periodic current satisfying continuity has the form $j(x,t)=J(t)-F(x,t)$, since $F(L,t)=0$. Winding then determines the previously unknown spatially constant current:
\[
 J(t)=\frac{2\pi k+\int_0^L F(x,t)/n(x,t)\,dx}
              {\int_0^L 1/n(x,t)\,dx}.
\]
The denominator is finite and strictly positive. Thus density plus winding fixes $S_x=j/n$. Integrating in $x$ fixes $S$ up to a function of time.

\paragraph{Recovering and checking the potential.}
The real part gives the explicit real potential
\[
 v(x,t)=-S_t(x,t)-\tfrac12 S_x(x,t)^2
             +\frac{\partial_x^2\sqrt{n(x,t)}}{2\sqrt{n(x,t)}}.
\]
Substitution of $\psi=\sqrt n e^{iS}$ verifies both real and imaginary parts of
$i\psi_t=-\tfrac12\psi_{xx}+v\psi$.
Because the winding $k$ is constant, $S_t$ is periodic, so $v$ is a potential on the circle. Replacing $S$ by $S+a(t)$ replaces $v$ by $v-a'(t)$, exactly the allowed time-dependent scalar gauge.

\paragraph{Compatibility and limitation.}
For reconstruction from a specified initial state, require $n(x,0)=|\psi_0(x)|^2$ and require the displayed reconstructed current at $t=0$ to equal $\operatorname{Im}(\overline{\psi_0}\partial_x\psi_0)$. These are genuine representability conditions; an arbitrary smooth density history need not satisfy the second one. With them, a constant phase choice matches $\psi_0$. Within this smooth positive one-particle class, any other solution with the same density and initial state has the same fixed winding, hence the same current and potential modulo gauge. Nodes invalidate division by $n$. For interacting many-particle states, the scalar density does not provide a single phase field with current $nS_x$, and internal stresses and correlations remain undetermined by this computation. Those are the first missing ingredients in the full source problem.

\paragraph{Verification and adversarial audit.}
Checked current integration and winding normalization, the quantum-pressure sign, periodicity of the potential, initial-current compatibility, and the precise failure at density zeros or many-body correlations. Model self-audit only; no independent human proof checking. Prompts A and B were applied to this canonical record and its qualified source scope. Checked current integration and winding normalization, the quantum-pressure sign, periodicity of the potential, initial-current compatibility, and the precise failure at density zeros or many-body correlations. Retained unresolved status and zero original-target progress; Replace the single-wave-function phase representation by a controlled interacting density-potential map, including representability, correlations, nodes and the required regularity class.

Coordinator checked the circle current reconstruction, winding constraint, potential formula and initial-state compatibility required for uniqueness modulo a time gauge. This additional check was model-assisted, not independent human verification.

\paragraph{Tools, process and contributions.}
Python 3 was used for file preparation and structural checks; no numerical experiment or theorem prover was used. Contribution prompt A was followed by prompt B. GPT-6 Astra with ultra reasoning drafted the research and performed its stated self-audit; any additional coordinator contribution is identified in the verification and audit. A GPT-6-based Codex coordinator assembled the manuscript and checked repository integration; its exact variant and reasoning configuration were not exposed. No human mathematical contribution, independent human proof check or formal proof certification is supplied. Complete derivations and the consulted source references are included here; private reasoning transcripts are not published.

